  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{49}=7,\\,b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{7}x.$",
